(function (T) { T.unit({ id: "7.5", title: "Inside the computer: hardware and software", q: "What are the parts of a computer and how do they work together?", lessons: [
{ t: "What counts as a computer?", o: "I can describe a device using input, process, output and storage.",
  k: ["hardware: the physical parts of a computer that you can touch", "software: the programs that tell the hardware what to do", "process: to carry out instructions on data"],
  d: ["Is a washing machine a computer? Give a reason.", "It contains one: it takes input from buttons and sensors, follows a program, and controls the motor and water."],
  s: ["A computer is...|A machine that takes input, processes it by following a program, and gives output|It can also store data|Phones, consoles, cars and washing machines all contain computers",
      "Input, process, output, storage|Input: data goes in (keyboard, sensor)|Process: the CPU follows instructions|Output: results come out (screen, speaker)|Storage: data is kept for later",
      "Hardware and software|Hardware: parts you can touch|Software: programs, which you cannot touch|Neither is any use without the other"],
  a: "For each of the eight devices on the sheet (phone, games console, cash machine, microwave, and others), complete an input-process-output-storage diagram. Support: sort the cards into the four boxes for the first three devices. Stretch: find a device at home that does not contain a computer and explain how you know.",
  w: [["What is hardware?", "The physical parts of a computer that you can touch."], ["What is software?", "The programs that tell the hardware what to do."], ["Name the four parts of the input-process-output-storage model.", "Input, process, output, storage."], ["For a cash machine, give one input and one output.", "Input: PIN typed on the keypad or the card. Output: cash, receipt or screen display."], ["Explain why a microwave oven counts as containing a computer.", "It takes input from buttons, follows a stored program to time and control the heating, and gives output on a display and beeper."]],
  x: ["Is a mouse hardware or software? Is a game hardware or software?", "Mouse: hardware. Game: software."] },
{ t: "Input devices and sensors", o: "I can identify input devices and explain what a sensor does.",
  k: ["input device: hardware that sends data into a computer", "sensor: an input device that measures something in the surroundings", "data: facts and figures before they are processed"],
  d: ["List every way you can give instructions to a phone.", "Touchscreen, buttons, microphone, camera, tilting it, fingerprint."],
  s: ["Input devices|Keyboard and mouse: text and pointing|Microphone, camera, scanner: sound and images|Touchscreen, game controller",
      "Sensors|Measure the real world automatically|Temperature, light, motion, pressure, moisture|No person is needed to enter the data",
      "Choosing an input|What kind of data is needed?|Who is using it, and where?|A supermarket uses a barcode scanner because it is fast and accurate"],
  a: "Match each of the 12 situations to the best input device or sensor and justify three of your choices. Support: choose from two options for each. Stretch: design the inputs for an automatic greenhouse and say what each sensor measures.",
  w: [["What is an input device?", "Hardware that sends data into a computer."], ["Name three input devices.", "Any three, for example keyboard, mouse, microphone, camera, scanner."], ["What does a sensor do?", "It measures something in the surroundings and sends the reading to the computer."], ["Which sensor would an automatic door use?", "A motion (or infrared) sensor."], ["Explain one advantage of a barcode scanner over typing in a product number.", "It is faster and avoids typing mistakes."]],
  x: ["Name one sensor in a phone and what it measures.", "For example a light sensor measuring brightness, or an accelerometer measuring movement."] },
{ t: "Lab: device hunt", o: "I can classify the devices around me as input, output or storage.",
  k: ["classify: to sort into groups", "peripheral: a device connected to a computer from outside", "port: a socket where a device plugs in"],
  d: ["Is a touchscreen an input device or an output device?", "Both: it shows the display and senses touch."],
  s: ["The hunt|Find 15 devices in the room and around school|Record each one in the table|Decide: input, output, storage, or more than one",
      "Tricky cases|Touchscreen: input and output|Headset with a microphone: input and output|USB stick: storage",
      "Peripherals and ports|A peripheral connects from outside|Look at how each connects: cable, wireless|Which port does it use?"],
  a: "In pairs, find and classify 15 devices using the hunt table, noting how each connects. Photograph or sketch three. Support: use the list of 15 devices to find. Stretch: find two devices that are both input and output, and explain each role.",
  w: [["What is a peripheral?", "A device connected to a computer from outside."], ["Classify: printer, webcam, USB stick.", "Output, input, storage."], ["Give one device that is both input and output.", "A touchscreen (or a headset with a microphone)."], ["What is a port?", "A socket where a device plugs in."], ["Explain why an interactive whiteboard is both an input and an output device.", "It displays the image (output) and senses the pen or finger touching it (input)."]],
  x: ["How many of your 15 devices were input, output and storage?", "Three totals."] },
{ t: "Output devices", o: "I can identify output devices and match them to a purpose.",
  k: ["output device: hardware that presents results from a computer", "resolution: the number of pixels on a screen", "actuator: an output device that produces movement"],
  d: ["Name three ways a computer can give you information without using a screen.", "Sound, printing, vibration, lights, movement."],
  s: ["Output devices|Screen and projector: images|Speakers and headphones: sound|Printer: paper copy; 3D printer: solid objects",
      "Actuators|Turn signals into movement|Motors, pumps, valves|A robot arm and an automatic door both use them",
      "Choosing an output|Who needs the information, and where?|A fire alarm uses a loud siren, not a screen|A blind user may need speech output"],
  a: "For each of the ten scenarios, choose the best output device and give a reason. Then complete the comparison table for screen, speaker and printer. Support: choose from the device cards. Stretch: explain which outputs a self-driving car needs, including actuators.",
  w: [["What is an output device?", "Hardware that presents results from a computer."], ["Name three output devices.", "Any three, for example screen, speaker, printer, projector."], ["What does an actuator do?", "It turns a signal from the computer into movement."], ["Which output device is best for a fire alarm? Why?", "A siren or loud speaker, because it must be noticed by everyone immediately."], ["Explain why a printer is still useful even though screens exist.", "It makes a permanent copy that needs no power and can be handed out, signed or posted."]],
  x: ["Give one actuator and what it moves.", "For example a motor that opens a door."] },
{ t: "Choosing devices for a user and a task", o: "I can choose suitable devices for a person and justify my choice.",
  k: ["justify: to give reasons for a choice", "requirement: something a user needs", "accessibility: making technology usable by everyone"],
  d: ["A graphic designer and a writer both need a computer. What might each need that the other does not?", "Designer: large accurate screen, drawing tablet. Writer: comfortable keyboard."],
  s: ["Start with the user|What will they do?|Where will they use it?|Do they have particular needs?",
      "Match device to requirement|Drawing: graphics tablet|Video calls: webcam and microphone|Limited hand movement: voice input or eye tracking",
      "Justify|Name the device|Say what it does|Say why that suits this user: 'because...'"],
  a: "Read the four user profiles and recommend two input devices and two output devices for each, with a 'because' for every choice. Support: complete the sentence frames. Stretch: one user has a limited budget. Decide which device to leave out and justify it.",
  w: [["What does justify mean?", "To give reasons for a choice."], ["Recommend an input device for an artist and justify it.", "A graphics tablet, because drawing with a pen is more natural and precise than with a mouse."], ["What is accessibility?", "Making technology usable by everyone, including people with disabilities."], ["Suggest an input method for a user who cannot use their hands.", "Voice recognition, eye tracking or a head pointer."], ["A student says 'The best computer has the most expensive devices.' Explain why this is wrong.", "The best choice meets the user's requirements. Expensive devices the user does not need waste money."]],
  x: ["Complete: A good recommendation names the device, says what it does and says ____.", "why it suits the user."] },
{ t: "Lab: design a setup for a user", o: "I can design a complete computer setup for a given user.",
  k: ["setup: all the devices a user needs, arranged to work together", "budget: the amount of money available", "trade-off: giving up one thing to gain another"],
  d: ["You have a limited budget for a setup. What do you decide first?", "What the user must be able to do: the essential requirements."],
  s: ["Your brief|Choose a user: gamer, architect, music producer or a wheelchair user working from home|List their requirements|Design the setup",
      "Present it|Draw and label the setup|Add a table: device, type, reason|Keep to the budget on the brief",
      "Trade-offs|A bigger screen may mean a cheaper keyboard|Explain what you gave up and why|No setup is perfect"],
  a: "Design and draw a labelled setup for your chosen user, with a justification table and a note about one trade-off you made. Support: choose devices from the catalogue sheet. Stretch: redesign the setup for use on a train.",
  w: [["Who is your user and what is their main task?", "A chosen user and task."], ["List the input devices you chose.", "A suitable list."], ["List the output devices you chose.", "A suitable list."], ["Justify your most important choice.", "A device with a reason linked to the user."], ["Describe one trade-off you made.", "What was given up and what was gained."]],
  x: ["What would you add first with a larger budget?", "A device with a reason."] },
{ t: "The CPU: the part that follows instructions", o: "I can describe the job of the CPU.",
  k: ["CPU: the central processing unit, which carries out the instructions of programs", "instruction: a single operation for the CPU to perform", "clock speed: how many cycles the CPU can perform each second"],
  d: ["Which part of a computer do you think does the 'thinking'?", "The CPU, also called the processor."],
  s: ["What the CPU does|It fetches an instruction from memory|It works out what the instruction means|It carries the instruction out, then repeats",
      "How fast?|Clock speed is measured in gigahertz (GHz)|3 GHz means 3 billion cycles each second|Each instruction is very simple: add, compare, copy",
      "A common mistake|The big box is the system unit, not the CPU|The CPU is a small chip inside, under a fan|It sits on the motherboard, which connects everything"],
  a: "Act out the CPU: in threes, one person is memory holding instruction cards, one is the CPU fetching and carrying them out, one is the output. Run the six-instruction program and record the result. Support: run the three-instruction program. Stretch: write your own six-instruction program for another group.",
  w: [["What does CPU stand for?", "Central processing unit."], ["What is the job of the CPU?", "To carry out the instructions of programs."], ["What unit is clock speed measured in?", "Gigahertz (GHz) (accept hertz)."], ["Put in order: carry out the instruction, fetch the instruction, work out what it means.", "Fetch, work out what it means, carry out."], ["A student points at the computer case and says 'That is the CPU.' Correct them.", "The case is the system unit. The CPU is a small chip inside it on the motherboard."]],
  x: ["What does a 2 GHz clock speed mean?", "About 2 billion cycles every second."] },
{ t: "RAM and ROM", o: "I can explain the difference between RAM and ROM.",
  k: ["RAM: memory that holds the programs and data in use; it is emptied when the power goes off", "ROM: memory that keeps its contents without power and holds the start-up instructions", "volatile: losing its contents when the power is switched off"],
  d: ["You are typing a document and the power goes off before you save. What happens to your work, and why?", "It is lost, because it was held in RAM, which needs power."],
  s: ["RAM|Holds the programs and data being used right now|Fast, and can be read and changed|Volatile: cleared when the power goes off",
      "ROM|Holds the instructions that start the computer|Cannot normally be changed|Non-volatile: keeps its contents without power",
      "Memory is not storage|RAM is like your desk: what you are working on now|Storage is like a cupboard: kept for later|More RAM lets more programs run at once"],
  a: "Sort the 16 statements into RAM, ROM or both, then write a paragraph explaining to a customer why adding RAM might speed up their computer. Support: sort the first eight statements. Stretch: explain what would happen at switch-on if a computer had no ROM.",
  w: [["What does volatile mean?", "The contents are lost when the power is switched off."], ["Which is volatile, RAM or ROM?", "RAM."], ["What is stored in ROM?", "The instructions needed to start the computer (the boot program)."], ["Give two differences between RAM and ROM.", "RAM is volatile and can be changed. ROM is non-volatile and cannot normally be changed."], ["Explain why unsaved work is lost in a power cut but saved work is not.", "Unsaved work is only in RAM, which is cleared without power. Saved work is on secondary storage, which keeps data without power."]],
  x: ["Complete: RAM is like a ____ and storage is like a ____.", "desk; cupboard."] },
{ t: "Lab: take apart a PC", o: "I can identify the main components inside a computer.",
  k: ["motherboard: the main circuit board that connects all the components", "component: one part of a computer", "heat sink: a metal block that carries heat away from the CPU"],
  d: ["Why is there a fan inside a computer?", "The components, especially the CPU, get hot and must be cooled."],
  s: ["Safety first|The computer is unplugged and has been for some time|Touch only what your teacher shows you|Handle boards by the edges",
      "What to find|Motherboard, CPU under its heat sink and fan|RAM sticks, storage drive, power supply|Ports on the back",
      "Record|Sketch or photograph each component|Label it and write its job in one sentence|Note what it connects to"],
  a: "In groups, examine the opened PC (or the photo set). Find, label and describe the job of the motherboard, CPU, RAM, storage drive, power supply and fan. Support: match the labelled photo cards to the real parts. Stretch: trace the route data takes from the storage drive to the screen.",
  w: [["What is the motherboard?", "The main circuit board that connects all the components."], ["What is a heat sink for?", "It carries heat away from the CPU."], ["Which component supplies electricity to the others?", "The power supply."], ["Where is the CPU found?", "On the motherboard, under the heat sink and fan."], ["Explain what would happen if the CPU fan stopped working.", "The CPU would overheat, so the computer would slow down or shut itself off to prevent damage."]],
  x: ["Name the five components you labelled.", "For example motherboard, CPU, RAM, storage drive, power supply."] },
{ t: "Secondary storage: magnetic, optical, solid state", o: "I can describe the three types of secondary storage.",
  k: ["secondary storage: where data and programs are kept when the power is off", "solid state: storage with no moving parts, using flash memory", "magnetic storage: storage that records data as magnetised areas on a spinning disk"],
  d: ["Where are your photos kept when your phone is switched off?", "In the phone's secondary storage (flash memory)."],
  s: ["Why it is needed|RAM is cleared when the power goes off|Secondary storage keeps files and programs|It is non-volatile",
      "Three types|Magnetic: hard disk drive, spinning platters, large and cheap|Optical: CD, DVD, Blu-ray, read with a laser|Solid state: SSD, USB stick, memory card, no moving parts",
      "How they differ|Solid state is fastest and toughest, but costs more per gigabyte|Magnetic gives the most capacity for the money|Optical is cheap to post but holds little"],
  a: "Complete the storage fact file for each type: how it stores data, an example device, one advantage and one disadvantage. Then sort ten devices into the three types. Support: match the fact cards to the types. Stretch: explain why phones use solid state storage and not hard disks.",
  w: [["Why does a computer need secondary storage?", "To keep data and programs when the power is off."], ["Name the three types of secondary storage.", "Magnetic, optical, solid state."], ["Give one example of each type.", "Hard disk drive; CD, DVD or Blu-ray; SSD, USB stick or memory card."], ["Which type has no moving parts?", "Solid state."], ["Explain why a hard disk drive is more easily damaged by dropping than an SSD.", "It has spinning platters and a moving read/write head, which can hit the disk. An SSD has no moving parts."]],
  x: ["Which type of storage is in a USB stick?", "Solid state."] },
{ t: "Comparing storage", o: "I can compare storage devices by capacity, speed, cost and durability.",
  k: ["capacity: how much data a device can hold", "durability: how well a device survives knocks and wear", "portable: easy to carry"],
  d: ["You need to give a friend 20 photos. How could you do it, and which way is best?", "USB stick, memory card, cloud link or email. The best depends on size and convenience."],
  s: ["Four ways to compare|Capacity: how much it holds|Speed: how quickly data is read and written|Cost: price for each gigabyte|Durability and portability",
      "Typical comparison|SSD: very fast, durable, higher cost|Hard disk: slower, large capacity, low cost|Optical disc: slow, small capacity, very cheap each",
      "Cloud storage|Files are kept on someone else's computers and reached over the internet|Available anywhere, easy to share|Needs a connection, and you rely on the provider"],
  a: "Complete the comparison table rating each storage type for capacity, speed, cost, durability and portability, then answer the 'which would you choose' questions with reasons. Support: use the ratings bank. Stretch: argue for and against relying only on cloud storage.",
  w: [["What is capacity?", "How much data a device can hold."], ["Which is usually faster, a hard disk drive or an SSD?", "An SSD."], ["Which gives the most storage for the least money?", "A hard disk drive."], ["Give one advantage and one disadvantage of cloud storage.", "Advantage: files can be reached from anywhere or shared easily. Disadvantage: it needs an internet connection, or you depend on the provider."], ["A photographer travels and needs to back up large photo files quickly. Recommend a device and justify it.", "A portable SSD: fast for large files, durable when carried, and small."]],
  x: ["Name the four ways to compare storage devices.", "Capacity, speed, cost, durability (accept portability)."] },
{ t: "Lab: storage recommendations", o: "I can recommend storage for a scenario and justify it.",
  k: ["scenario: a described situation", "backup: a second copy of data kept in case the first is lost", "recommend: to suggest the best choice"],
  d: ["Why should important files be kept in more than one place?", "If one copy is lost, damaged or stolen, another still exists."],
  s: ["Read the scenario|Who is the user?|How much data, and what kind?|Does it need to be carried, shared or kept for years?",
      "Decide|Pick the type that fits best|Check it against capacity, speed, cost and durability|Think about a backup",
      "Write it up|Recommendation|Two reasons linked to the scenario|One drawback and how to manage it"],
  a: "For each of the three scenarios (a school's CCTV recordings, a film student's projects, a family's photos), write a recommendation with two reasons and one drawback. Support: use the recommendation frame. Stretch: design a backup plan for one scenario using two different storage types.",
  w: [["What is a backup?", "A second copy of data kept in case the first is lost."], ["Recommend storage for a school keeping months of CCTV video.", "Large hard disk drives, because huge capacity is needed at low cost and speed matters less."], ["Recommend storage for moving a presentation between school and home.", "A USB stick or cloud storage, because it is portable and the file is small."], ["Give one drawback of your CCTV recommendation.", "Hard disks have moving parts and can fail, so a backup is needed."], ["Explain why a good backup is kept in a different place from the original.", "A fire, flood or theft could destroy both copies if they are together."]],
  x: ["Name two storage types you would combine for a safe backup.", "For example an external drive and cloud storage."] },
{ t: "System software and the operating system", o: "I can state what an operating system does.",
  k: ["operating system: software that manages the hardware and lets programs run", "system software: software that runs and looks after the computer itself", "user interface: the way a person interacts with the computer"],
  d: ["Name an operating system you have used.", "For example Windows, macOS, Android, iOS, ChromeOS, Linux."],
  s: ["What an operating system is|The software that runs the computer|It loads when the computer starts|Every other program relies on it",
      "Its main jobs|Provides a user interface|Manages files and folders|Manages memory and running programs|Controls devices such as the printer and keyboard",
      "Why it is needed|Without it each program would have to control the hardware itself|It lets many programs share the computer|It keeps users' accounts and files secure"],
  a: "Match the 12 everyday actions (saving a file, plugging in a mouse, switching between apps, logging in, and others) to the operating system job that makes them possible. Support: use the four job cards. Stretch: compare two operating systems you have used: two similarities and two differences.",
  w: [["What is an operating system?", "Software that manages the hardware and lets programs run."], ["Name two operating systems.", "Any two, for example Windows, macOS, Android, iOS, Linux."], ["State three jobs of an operating system.", "Any three: user interface, file management, memory management, managing devices, security."], ["Which job of the operating system is used when you create a folder?", "File management."], ["Explain what would happen if two programs tried to use the printer at once and there was no operating system.", "Their output could be mixed up. The operating system queues the jobs so they print one at a time."]],
  x: ["What is a user interface?", "The way a person interacts with the computer."] },
{ t: "Application and utility software", o: "I can tell system software from application software.",
  k: ["application software: programs that let a user do a task, such as writing or drawing", "utility software: small programs that help look after the computer", "install: to add software to a computer so it can be used"],
  d: ["Sort into two groups: word processor, Windows, game, antivirus.", "Tasks for the user: word processor, game. Looking after the computer: Windows, antivirus."],
  s: ["Two families|System software runs and maintains the computer|Application software does jobs for the user|An app cannot run without system software",
      "Application software|Word processor, spreadsheet, web browser|Image editor, game, music player|Chosen by what the user wants to do",
      "Utility software|Antivirus: finds and removes harmful programs|Backup: makes copies of files|Compression: makes files smaller"],
  a: "Sort the 20 software cards into application, operating system or utility, then recommend three applications and one utility for a school office. Support: sort into two groups (application or system) first. Stretch: explain why a web browser is an application even though almost everyone uses one.",
  w: [["What is application software?", "Programs that let a user do a task."], ["Give three examples of application software.", "Any three, for example word processor, spreadsheet, browser, game."], ["What is utility software?", "Small programs that help look after the computer."], ["Classify: antivirus, spreadsheet, Android.", "Utility, application, operating system."], ["Explain why application software needs an operating system.", "The application asks the operating system to use the hardware, files and memory. It cannot control them by itself."]],
  x: ["Give one utility and what it does.", "For example antivirus, which finds and removes harmful programs."] },
{ t: "Lab: the operating system as a restaurant manager", o: "I can model how an operating system shares resources between programs.",
  k: ["resource: something programs need, such as memory, processor time or a device", "multitasking: running several programs so they seem to work at the same time", "queue: a line of jobs waiting their turn"],
  d: ["A restaurant has one chef and five tables ordering at once. How does everyone get fed?", "The chef works on each order a little at a time, or in a queue, organised by a manager."],
  s: ["The model|Chef = CPU|Customers = programs|Kitchen worktop = RAM|Manager = operating system",
      "What the manager does|Decides which order the chef works on next|Gives each order space on the worktop|Queues orders for the single oven (a device)",
      "What we learn|The chef switches so fast that every table thinks it is being served|Too many orders and the worktop fills up|Then everything slows down"],
  a: "Role-play the restaurant in groups of six for three rounds: two customers, then four, then six with a small worktop. After each round record what happened and what the manager had to do. Support: use the role cards with scripts. Stretch: write what each part of the model stands for and one way the model is not like a real computer.",
  w: [["In the model, what does the chef stand for?", "The CPU."], ["What does the manager stand for?", "The operating system."], ["What is multitasking?", "Running several programs so they seem to work at the same time."], ["What happened when the worktop was full, and what does that mean in a real computer?", "Work slowed or stopped. In a computer, RAM is full so programs run slowly."], ["Explain how one CPU can appear to run several programs at once.", "It switches between them very quickly, giving each a short turn."]],
  x: ["Name two resources an operating system shares between programs.", "Any two: processor time, memory, devices, storage."] },
{ t: "Revision: build a paper computer", o: "I can recall the components of a computer and how they connect.",
  k: ["revise: to go over learning again", "connect: to join so that data can pass", "function: the job something does"],
  d: ["List as many internal components as you can in one minute.", "CPU, RAM, ROM, motherboard, storage drive, power supply, fan."],
  s: ["Hardware|Input, output and storage devices|CPU, RAM and ROM|Motherboard and power supply",
      "Memory and storage|RAM: volatile, in use now|ROM: non-volatile, start-up|Secondary storage: magnetic, optical, solid state",
      "Software|Operating system: manages everything|Utilities: maintain|Applications: do tasks for the user"],
  a: "Build a paper computer: cut out and arrange the component cards on the motherboard sheet, draw the connections, and write each component's function beside it. Then answer the revision questions. Support: place the cards using the outline. Stretch: add software layers above the hardware and show which talks to which.",
  w: [["Give the function of the CPU.", "It carries out the instructions of programs."], ["Give two differences between RAM and secondary storage.", "RAM is volatile and holds what is in use now. Secondary storage is non-volatile and keeps data for later."], ["Name the three types of secondary storage with an example of each.", "Magnetic (hard disk), optical (DVD), solid state (SSD or USB stick)."], ["Classify: keyboard, speaker, SSD, Windows, spreadsheet.", "Input, output, storage, operating system, application."], ["Explain what happens, in order, from pressing the power button to opening a game.", "ROM starts the computer; the operating system loads from storage into RAM; the user chooses the game; the game loads from storage into RAM; the CPU runs it."]],
  x: ["Which topic do you need to revise most before the assessment?", "A named topic."] },
{ t: "Assessed task: specify a computer and justify", o: "I can specify a computer for a user and justify every choice.",
  k: ["specification: a list of exactly what a computer should have", "justify: to give reasons", "terminology: the correct technical words"],
  d: ["What three things must each line of your specification include?", "The component, your choice, and the reason."],
  s: ["The task|You are given a user and their needs|Specify: input devices, output devices, CPU, RAM, storage, software|Work on your own",
      "Success criteria|Every choice suits the user|Every choice has a reason|Correct terminology is used throughout",
      "Good justification|Weak: 'An SSD because it is good'|Strong: 'An SSD because it loads large video files quickly and survives being carried'|Link each reason to the user"],
  a: "Write a full specification for the user on your task card, with a justification for each component and one trade-off. Support: use the specification table with prompts. Stretch: write a second, cheaper specification and explain what the user loses.",
  w: [["Who is your user and what are their two main needs?", "A user with two needs."], ["State and justify your choice of storage.", "A storage type with a reason linked to the user."], ["State and justify your choice of input devices.", "Suitable devices with reasons."], ["Name the operating system and two applications you chose, with reasons.", "Suitable software with reasons."], ["Describe one trade-off in your specification.", "What was given up and why."]],
  x: ["Which justification are you most confident about?", "One choice."] },
{ t: "Feedback and reflection", o: "I can use feedback to improve my specification and reflect on my learning.",
  k: ["feedback: comments that help you improve", "improve: to make better", "target: a specific thing to work on next"],
  d: ["What makes a justification strong?", "It links the choice to what this particular user needs."],
  s: ["Read your feedback|Find your strength and your next step|Check your level for each outcome|Ask about anything unclear",
      "Improve|Rewrite your weakest justification|Fix any incorrect terminology|Use a different colour",
      "Reflect|What can I do now that I could not do before?|Which idea was hardest: RAM, storage or software?|What question do I still have?"],
  a: "Improve your specification using the feedback and complete the unit reflection. Support: your teacher has marked the one justification to rewrite. Stretch: explain how today's phones fit the input-process-output-storage model.",
  w: [["Copy your next step and say what you changed.", "The feedback and the change."], ["Rewrite your weakest justification.", "An improved justification linked to the user."], ["Name one thing you can do now that you could not do six weeks ago.", "A unit skill."], ["Which idea did you find hardest, and what helped?", "A reasoned answer."], ["Set one target for the next unit.", "A specific target."]],
  x: ["In one sentence, how do the parts of a computer work together?", "Input devices send data to the CPU, which follows software instructions held in memory, stores results and sends them to output devices."] }
] }); })(typeof module !== "undefined" ? require("../lessons.js") : window.Toolkit);
